body:prose = >`
  &ND v2

  # Aeonic Exact Arithmetic v1 Snapshot 0.1 [# aeonic-exact-arithmetic-v1-snapshot-0-1]

  Status: immutable compatibility snapshot aeonic-arithmetic-specs-v1-snapshot-0.1,
  frozen 2026-11-12. Proposal lifecycle and disabled publication are preserved.
  This pins the finite exact arithmetic/materialization contract; comparison
  claims and deferred operations retain their existing scope. The explicit
  snapshot test allowances do not prescribe host defaults or machine guarantees.

  The operation schema is [@ /artifacts/assets/aeonic-operations/v1/snapshots/0.1/arithmetic-contract.schema.json | arithmetic snapshot 0.1 schema].

  ## 1. Scope and numerical meaning [# 1-scope-and-numerical-meaning]

  The required surface consists of binary [$ add], [$ subtract], [$ multiply], and
  unary [$ materialize]. Arithmetic is exact for finite AEON Number operands and
  exact derived results. Number integer, decimal and exponent representations
  share this numerical domain. All four authored/derived binary pairings are
  admitted; no operand-family promotion or output family is selected by arithmetic.

  Division, quantization, rounding, aggregates, radix arithmetic, infinity, NaN,
  temporal operations, implicit coercion and expression syntax are deferred.
  An unsupported operation or domain returns a diagnostic. No automatic string,
  Boolean, symbol, null or container conversion is performed.

  The exact value is a normalized coefficient/exponent pair denoting
  [$ coefficient * 10^exponent]. Both integers use ASCII decimal text. Nonzero
  coefficients have an optional minus sign, no leading or trailing zeros, and no
  plus sign. Exponents have an optional minus sign, no leading zeros or plus
  sign, and encode zero as [$ 0]. Mathematical zero is exactly [$ (0, 0)].
  Incoming derived pairs must already be normalized. Authored Number spellings
  remain preserved at their boundary and are decoded without host floating point.

  All calculated zeros have one mathematical value and no arithmetic sign.
  Arithmetic does not recover a source spelling, source scale or source accuracy
  for a derived result. A finite decimal result is closed under the three required
  arithmetic operations, subject to resource limits; rational intermediates are
  not needed for this scope.

  ## 2. Candidate portable boundary [# 2-candidate-portable-boundary]

  Requests contain exactly [$ operation] and [$ input]. Binary inputs contain required
  [$ left] and [$ right], with optional [$ context] and [$ policy]. Materialization inputs
  contain required [$ value], with optional [$ context] and required [$ policy].
  All these objects are closed. Native APIs may use immutable value objects or
  handles while exposing equivalent logical data through their adapters.
  Successful result bundles, number descriptors, metadata and effective-policy
  objects are also closed to fields outside their defined shapes.

  An operand is a closed bundle containing [$ number] and [$ history]. [$ number] is
  one of the following closed descriptors:

  ~~~$ json
  {"category":"finiteNumber","value":"1.0"}
  ~~~$

  ~~~$ json
  {"category":"exactNumber","coefficient":"1","exponent":"0"}
  ~~~$

  The finite-Number payload grammar is the existing comparison boundary's exact
  numerical text grammar: optional sign; integer, decimal or leading-dot decimal;
  optional signed decimal exponent. It admits [$ 1], [$ 1.0], [$ 1e0] and [$ .5];
  underscores belong to source parsing and must be removed by the source adapter
  before this boundary, preserving the source separately. Native numeric payloads
  are not accepted in place of text.

  [$ history] has the symbolic wire labels [$ none-declared] and
  [$ approximation-declared]. It is required for derived inputs. For authored
  finite-Number inputs it may be omitted, meaning [$ none-declared]. Every successful
  result includes it explicitly. This prevents directly chaining a derived result
  while silently defaulting its missing history.

  A complete successful arithmetic result has exactly these outer fields:

  ~~~$ json
  {
    "outcome":"value",
    "value":{
      "number":{"category":"exactNumber","coefficient":"2","exponent":"0"},
      "history":"none-declared"
    },
    "metadata":{
      "evaluation":"exact-for-operands",
      "effectivePolicy":{"profile":"aeonic.operations.arithmetic.exact.v1"}
    }
  }
  ~~~$

  The complete [$ value] bundle can be supplied as the next operation's operand.
  Materialization returns the same envelope and bundle shape, with a [$ finiteNumber]
  descriptor instead of an [$ exactNumber] descriptor and complete materialization
  settings in [$ metadata.effectivePolicy]. Thus history travels with the number
  without introducing an AEON literal or attribute.

  Recognized deferred categories are [$ string], [$ boolean], [$ symbol],
  [$ positiveInfinity], [$ negativeInfinity], [$ nan], [$ explicitNull], [$ explicitAbsence],
  [$ toggle], [$ hex], [$ radix], [$ encoding], [$ separator], [$ sansaAddress], [$ referenceForm],
  [$ temporal] and [$ container]. Their [$ number] descriptor payload is opaque beyond
  the category, matching the comparison contract's deferred-domain approach.
  The containing bundle and its history are still validated. An unknown category
  is malformed input, not a new optional capability.

  ## 3. Approximation history [# 3-approximation-history]

  History records a declaration about the supplied values or their processing
  path. It does not claim a numerical error bound, identify a rounding mode,
  track correlation, certify measurement accuracy or reconstruct an earlier value.
  [$ none-declared] means that no history was declared; it does not certify that
  the original quantity was exact.

  If either operand carries [$ approximation-declared], the result does too.
  Otherwise it carries [$ none-declared]. Materialization copies the status without
  clearing it. These rules apply to zero, multiplication by zero and cancellation.
  The calculation remains exact for its numerical operands. For example, a marked
  [$ 0.33] multiplied by [$ 3] yields exact [$ 0.99] with the marker retained.

  Detailed provenance is deferred. A standard AEON document annotation is also
  deferred: applications must retain the bundle or explicitly map its history
  when storing AEON. Exporting only the bare Number loses that metadata; a later
  import must not invent a missing declaration.

  ## 4. Policies and composition [# 4-policies-and-composition]

  The proposed arithmetic profile identifier is
  [$ aeonic.operations.arithmetic.exact.v1]. It is the operation contract's baseline
  selection, not a registered profile or a general-purpose rounding policy.

  [$ context] permits only an optional nonempty string [$ profile]. Binary [$ policy]
  permits only that same field. Materialization [$ policy] permits optional
  [$ profile], required [$ family] ([$ integer], [$ decimal] or [$ exponent]), and optional
  [$ zeroSign] ([$ positive] or [$ negative], default [$ positive]). There is no implicit
  family default and no fallback between families. Negative zero is requested
  explicitly; the setting affects only zero, never the sign of a nonzero result.

  Validate all supplied configuration shapes before resolving the effective
  profile. Per-operation [$ policy.profile] overrides [$ context.profile]; absent both,
  use the baseline identifier. Resolve only the effective profile, so a syntactically
  valid overridden profile does not require an unavailable resolver. Malformed
  overridden configuration still fails. No file, network, database, callback,
  locale or process-environment lookup is implicit.

  Resource limits are trusted host/adapter settings, outside the logical request.
  An input cannot enlarge its own resource allowance. Invalid trusted settings
  are API/harness failures, not operation diagnostics. Unknown limit names,
  non-object settings including explicit null, and values other than nonnegative
  integers are invalid. An omitted limit uses its configured default. Limits may
  be explicitly lowered for a test; that does not establish advertised capacity.
  Host Booleans are not integers for this configuration contract. Profile IDs and
  operation names must be nonempty Unicode-scalar strings; malformed strings
  fail configuration or envelope validation before availability is considered.

  Comparison policies do not automatically apply to arithmetic or materialization.
  Arithmetic settings do not alter comparison semantics. Passing a materialized
  Number to the frozen comparison API requires its existing descriptor shape;
  that comparison does not assess or propagate arithmetic history. Consumers
  retain history separately rather than attributing new behavior to snapshot 0.1.

  ## 5. Exact materialization [# 5-exact-materialization]

  Materialization accepts either supported operand kind and preserves its exact
  value and history. It does not apply schema constraints, round, truncate, pad to
  a requested business scale, or infer an output family from an operand.

  For normalized coefficient [$ c] and exponent [$ e]:

  | Family | Rule |
  | <-- | <-- |
  | [$ integer] | Zero emits [$ 0]. A nonzero value requires [$ e >= 0]; append [$ e] zeros to [$ c]. A negative exponent is [$ unrepresentable_result]. |
  | [$ decimal] | Expand the exact decimal point position. Include at least one digit on each side of the point; integral values end in [$ .0]. No redundant fractional trailing zeros are added. |
  | [$ exponent] | Emit the normalized coefficient, lowercase [$ e], and the normalized exponent, including [$ e0]. For example [$ (123, -2)] emits [$ 123e-2]. |

  For zero, explicit [$ zeroSign: "negative"] produces [$ -0], [$ -0.0] or [$ -0e0] in
  the selected family. Otherwise the corresponding positive representation is
  used. These renderings follow existing AEON canonical family rules; the
  calculation's coefficient/exponent normalization is a separate operation.

  Determine representability and output length before allocating expanded text.
  If an exact fractional result cannot be represented as an integer, return
  [$ unrepresentable_result] with no value. If a valid rendering exceeds its output
  budget, return [$ resource_limit], with no truncated or alternative rendering.
  The effective family and zero-sign setting are included in successful metadata.

  Schema validation belongs to the consumer/AEOS after successful calculation
  and materialization. For example, [$ 127 + 1] may materialize as [$ 128], which an
  [$ int8] destination rejects separately. A failed materialization does not return
  its candidate Number; a previously completed exact result remains available
  only if the caller already retained that separate successful result.

  ## 6. Deterministic validation and diagnostics [# 6-deterministic-validation-and-diagnostics]

  Stop at the first failed stage/substep. Return exactly [$ outcome: "diagnostic"],
  [$ reason] and [$ context], with optional human-readable [$ message]. Context has
  [$ stage] and an operation-local JSON Pointer [$ path], or null when unavailable.
  There is no [$ value], partial value, automatic null, warning-based success or
  fallback profile on failure. Message text is non-normative.

  | Stage | Fixed checks and failure |
  | <-- | <-- |
  | 1 | Request byte/depth guards before deeper processing: [$ resource_limit], null path. |
  | 2 | Strict JSON decoding, duplicate keys, request/input object shapes, required [$ operation]/[$ input], closed generic input fields; then operation availability; then known-operation arity fields. [$ invalid_operand] or [$ unsupported_operation]. |
  | 3 | Context shape, then policy shape and required materialization family; validate configuration field values; resolve effective profile. [$ invalid_policy], then [$ unsupported_profile] as applicable. |
  | 4 | Validate operand bundles and supported descriptor payloads, left before right, or the materialization value. [$ invalid_operand]; a reached operand-size/exponent guard is [$ resource_limit]. |
  | 5 | Domain support, left before right. Recognized deferred categories yield [$ unsupported_domain]. Every pairing of the two supported kinds is compatible. |
  | 6 | Arithmetic workspace guard; exact evaluation; normalized result coefficient limit, then result exponent limit. Reached limits yield [$ resource_limit], null path. Materialization skips arithmetic evaluation. |
  | 7 | Materialization representability, then emitted text-length guard, then rendering. [$ unrepresentable_result], then [$ resource_limit], both with null path. |

  Within a closed object, check required fields in their listed order before
  unknown fields. Select unknown fields in decoded Unicode-scalar order rather
  than host map order. An unrepresentable pointer, including an isolated surrogate
  in an unknown field name, yields null. All malformed JSON values are admitted
  only as failure inputs, never as numbers through a host decoder.

  At stage 2 the generic input field vocabulary is [$ left], [$ right], [$ value],
  [$ context], [$ policy]. A nonempty unknown operation is rejected before applying a
  known operation's arity. Known binary operations require [$ left], then [$ right],
  and reject [$ value]; [$ materialize] requires [$ value] and rejects [$ left]/[$ right].
  Its missing or malformed policy is diagnosed at stage 3, not as operand arity.

  For each stage-4 bundle, require a [$ number] object and recognized string
  category, check bundle fields, validate history, then validate the supported
  descriptor. Finite payload order is [$ value]; derived order is [$ coefficient],
  then [$ exponent], then pair normalization, including zero's exponent. Size guards
  on supplied text precede its detailed grammar checks. Required derived history
  and unknown descriptor fields are shape failures before payload parsing.
  An unsupported descriptor's payload remains opaque, but a malformed right
  supported operand at stage 4 still precedes a left unsupported domain at stage 5.

  Diagnostic precedence examples:

  | Competing failures | Required diagnostic |
  | <-- | <-- |
  | Unsupported profile and malformed numerical payload | [$ unsupported_profile], stage 3, [$ /input/policy/profile] when selected there |
  | Missing materialization family and unsupported profile | [$ invalid_policy], stage 3, [$ /input/policy/family] |
  | Malformed left and right operands | Left [$ invalid_operand], stage 4 |
  | Recognized unsupported left domain and malformed right finite payload | Right [$ invalid_operand], stage 4 |
  | Two recognized unsupported domains | Left [$ unsupported_domain], stage 5 |
  | Fractional value requested as integer and zero output allowance | [$ unrepresentable_result], stage 7 |
  | Admission limit reached and unsupported operation/profile | Admission [$ resource_limit], stage 1 |

  Logical JSON rejects duplicate member names at every depth and non-JSON numeric
  constants. Native adapters must reject cycles, guard depth before recursive
  serialization and avoid exponential traversal of shared acyclic subgraphs.
  The portable stage ordering above applies to logical JSON requests, including
  malformed requests expressible as JSON. Native graphs containing cycles are
  outside that representation. A native adapter must fail closed: it reports
  [$ invalid_operand] for a detected cycle, or [$ resource_limit] if a byte/depth
  guard is reached first. No cross-adapter traversal order or tie-break is claimed
  for competing failures in such non-JSON graphs. Finite acyclic shared graphs
  remain subject to per-path depth and, at structured JSON ingress, expanded JSON
  byte limits; a completed shallow visit cannot waive a deeper path's guard.
  Passing the logical CTS does not establish native graph hardening or any bound
  on host getters, proxies or user-defined iterators. Those require separate
  implementation evidence. Machine-dependent timeout is a harness failure, not a
  portable diagnostic.

  ## 7. Resource model [# 7-resource-model]

  Limits apply per operation; applications own whole-chain limits. Compact exact
  results and materialized text have separate limits. Subject to other applicable
  bounds, [$ (1, 1000000)] can remain compact even when integer materialization
  would require 1,000,001 digits and fail its output budget. Exponent rendering
  can still succeed in a separate operation with a suitable output allowance.

  The snapshot CTS selects the following explicit test configuration; these are
  not required native defaults or a machine-time/memory guarantee:

  | Trusted limit | Candidate default | Counting rule |
  | <-- | <-- | <-- |
  | [$ maxRequestBytes] | 4,194,304 | Actual UTF-8 logical JSON bytes, excluding the CTS envelope; no reserialization for text input. |
  | [$ maxRequestDepth] | 32 | Nested object/array levels; root container is level one. |
  | [$ maxNumericCharacters] | 65,536 | Per operand: authored numeric text, or coefficient plus exponent text, including signs. |
  | [$ maxCoefficientDigits] | 65,536 | Per decoded operand and normalized result, excluding a minus sign; zero counts as one digit. |
  | [$ maxExponentMagnitude] | 1,000,000 | Absolute authored exponent, normalized operand exponent and normalized result exponent. Check authored exponent even for zero. |
  | [$ maxWorkspaceDigits] | 131,072 | Deterministic arithmetic workspace admission described below. |
  | [$ maxOutputCharacters] | 65,536 | Emitted Number literal text, including signs, point, [$ e] and exponent. Bundle/metadata bytes are excluded. |

  These numbers are provisional engineering recommendations, not advertised
  implementation capacities or frozen support floors. Calibrate on both runtimes
  before a conformance claim or snapshot. A maximum for each dimension does not
  promise every Cartesian product of all maxima. Smaller explicit limits are
  valid negative-test settings and must not silently enable approximation.

  Workspace admission is independent of an implementation's private allocation
  strategy. Addition/subtraction with zero, multiplication by zero, and exact
  additive cancellation are recognized first using normalized operands. They
  need no expanded workspace. Multiplication by one does not bypass the declared
  workspace admission bound merely because an implementation can optimize it.
  For remaining addition/subtraction, let [$ m] be the smaller exponent; required
  workspace is the larger of [$ digits(c_left) + e_left - m] and
  [$ digits(c_right) + e_right - m]. For nonzero multiplication it is
  [$ digits(c_left) + digits(c_right)]. Reject if this exceeds
  [$ maxWorkspaceDigits] before constructing aligned integers or their product.
  This is an explicit conservative admission bound, not a claim about actual
  machine work or memory. Result limits still apply after normalization, including
  carry and trailing-zero removal. Identity/zero shortcuts do not skip operand
  validation, domain checks or final result limits.

  Additional deterministic implementation-specific execution budgets may be
  advertised separately. They do not redefine standardized digit counts, justify
  silently omitting a required operation or establish semantic conformance merely
  by returning [$ resource_limit]. Positive conformance cases run with adequate
  declared allowances; work-budget rejection is tested separately. Unexpected
  allocation failures and crashes remain harness failures.

  ## 8. Capability and conformance boundary [# 8-capability-and-conformance-boundary]

  The initial capability is the complete finite exact arithmetic/materialization
  surface; it does not have per-operation semantic opt-outs. Implementations
  declare their supported limits and any optional work budget separately from
  this capability. No arithmetic capability follows from comparison conformance.

  Shared conformance must check exact normalized values, history, complete
  effective policy, selected output family, diagnostics and precedence. It must
  include all authored/derived operand pairings, equivalent numerical spellings,
  signed-zero inputs, very large exact values, non-normalized derived inputs,
  chaining, malformed/unknown fields, competing failures and independently
  generated resource boundaries.

  Arithmetic law tests cover commutativity of add/multiply, subtraction sign
  relations, identities and distributivity only where all participating operations
  are admitted within their applicable budgets. History follows its own union
  rule even when an algebraic identity eliminates numerical dependence. A finite
  test suite is evidence, not a proof over all input magnitudes.

  The separate aeonic-arithmetic CTS snapshot 0.1 owns executable operation,
  chaining, law and generated-boundary cases. Its immutable manifest is the
  compatibility target; the separate mutable next target is development evidence.

  A named snapshot must pin its own source, schema, binding and suite bytes.
  Its complete trusted resource-limit map is supplied explicitly for each CTS
  invocation; individual boundary cases may select documented companion limits.
  This configuration is part of the claim, not an inference about native defaults
  or every Cartesian combination of maxima. All four operations, both operand
  kinds and the history/materialization rules remain required; a capacity setting
  cannot be presented as a semantic opt-out. Native-adapter checks and optional
  implementation work budgets must be reported separately. A passing snapshot
  does not publish this proposal or release an implementation package.

`
